    "id": "p40-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "[Newton’s law of cooling] A container of water, originally just below boiling point at $96^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$, is placed in a fridge whose temperature is $0^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$. The container is known to cool in such a way that its temperature halves every 20 minutes.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p40-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain why the temperature $T^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$ after $n$ hours in the fridge is given by the function $T=96\\times\\left(\\frac12\\right)^{3n}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "There are $3n$ periods of 20 minutes in $n$ hours. Starting at $96^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$ and halving each period gives $T=96\\times\\left(\\frac12\\right)^{3n}$.",
          "worked": "In $n$ hours there are $60n$ minutes, so the number of 20-minute periods is $\\frac{60n}{20}=3n$. The initial temperature is $96^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$ and each period multiplies it by $\\frac12$, giving $T=96\\times\\left(\\frac12\\right)^{3n}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
